<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Local executor for the host-input loop.

  Deliberately launchd rather than GitHub Actions. Hourly execution is roughly
  730 runs a month, which against a 2000-minute allowance is most of the
  budget for one repository. A loop that depends on a budget stops when the
  budget does, and this one is meant to run unattended.

  The remote MUST be SSH, not HTTPS. Under launchd there is no terminal, so
  an interactive credential helper (git-credential-manager, for one) returns
  nothing and git falls back to anonymous access. Against a private repo that
  surfaces as "Repository not found", which reads like a missing repo rather
  than a failed login, and the pull and push both fail silently while the
  cycle itself appears to succeed:

      git remote set-url origin git@github.com:OWNER/REPO.git

  Verify before trusting the schedule:
      launchctl kickstart -k gui/$(id -u)/com.sovereign.hostcycle
      tail ~/Library/Logs/sovereign-research-hostcycle.log

  Install into a dedicated clone that is isolated from developer branches:
      bash ops/install_hostcycle.sh

  Check what it did:
      tail ~/Library/Logs/sovereign-research-hostcycle.log
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.sovereign.hostcycle</string>

  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/bash</string>
    <string>REPLACE_WITH_EXECUTOR_REPO_PATH/ops/run_hostcycle.sh</string>
    <string>REPLACE_WITH_EXECUTOR_REPO_PATH</string>
  </array>

  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>HOSTCYCLE_MAX_JITTER_SECONDS</key>
    <string>150</string>
    <key>PYTHON_BIN</key>
    <string>REPLACE_WITH_PYTHON_BIN</string>
  </dict>

  <!-- Poll, do not predict. The host's cadence is not guaranteed hourly and
       may drift or change, so scheduling against a guessed wall clock means
       a cycle can sit undrained for an hour whenever the guess is wrong.
       Draining every ten minutes removes the need to know the schedule at
       all: whenever an input lands, it executes within ten minutes.

       This is only affordable because an idle pass is genuinely idle. The
       run is a few seconds, it appends nothing, and it rewrites no file, so
       it produces no commit. That property is load-bearing: when feedback
       was rewritten unconditionally, every pass pushed a one-line change to
       its own timestamp and buried the real receipts.

       The leading random sleep of up to 150 seconds keeps the job off a
       fixed wall-clock boundary, so repeated runs do not land on the same
       second as the host's own commit and lose a race to a mid-push tree. -->
  <key>StartInterval</key>
  <integer>600</integer>

  <!-- Three failures used to be invisible here.

       A failed pull was followed by a semicolon, so the executor ran anyway
       against a stale tree and produced a receipt from an out-of-date view.
       It now refuses.

       A failed push was swallowed: the push sat inside a `||` block whose
       status nobody read, and the job exited with the EXECUTOR's code, so a
       receipt that never reached the remote reported success.

       An idle pass never retried a commit that had been made but not pushed,
       so one network blip stranded a receipt on this machine forever. The
       push is now driven by "is the local branch ahead", not by "did this
       pass produce something", which retries any pending publication.

       The receipt is committed and pushed in the same step. Writing it and
       stopping leaves a dirty journal that the next pull rebases over, and
       the remote never receives the receipt at all, so the loop produces
       evidence nobody outside this machine can see. -->

  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>REPLACE_WITH_HOSTCYCLE_LOG_PATH</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>REPLACE_WITH_HOSTCYCLE_LOG_PATH</string>

  <!-- Not RunAtLoad: loading the agent should not execute a cycle as a side
       effect of installing it. -->
  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <false/>
</dict>
</plist>
